smd - a small markdown (and barely-html) viewer
=================================================
(formerly surf, a webkit browser - this is a full repurposing, not
a fork of the browser code. see below.)

smd renders markdown straight to a window: headings, bold/italic,
inline & fenced code, links, blockquotes, lists, hr, images, and
LaTeX math (inline `$..$` and block `$$..$$`, rasterized through
pdflatex+pdftoppm if you have them installed).

There's no webkit, no javascript, no network stack. Rendering is
GTK + cairo + pango directly, which is a fraction of the dependency
weight and a fraction of the attack surface. If you just want to
read/preview a markdown file, you don't need a browser engine for
that.

It also does a bare-bones job of plain html - just enough tag
recognition (headings, p, b/i, code/pre, a, ul/li, blockquote, hr,
img) to eyeball a document while you're writing it. No CSS. If you
need a real browser, use one.

Point smd at a file and it watches it with inotify - edit in your
editor, see the render update in the same window, no manual reload.


why the rewrite
----------------
surf is a fine minimal browser, but webkit is enormous and none of
that machinery is needed just to read markdown. Ripping it out and
building a small renderer directly on cairo/pango keeps the "do one
thing" spirit while actually being small.


requirements
-------------
- gtk+3
- pdflatex + pdftoppm (optional - only needed for math; smd falls
  back to showing the raw latex source if they're not installed)
- xdg-open (optional - only needed for clicking links)


installation
-------------
Edit config.mk to match your setup, then:

    cp config.def.h config.h   # (make does this for you too)
    make
    sudo make install

Default PREFIX is /usr/local.


running
--------

    smd notes.md
    smd README.html


configuration
--------------
smd is configured via config.h (colors, fonts, margins, keybindings),
copied from config.def.h at build time, dwm/suckless-style. There's
no config.h left checked in on purpose - edit your own, rebuild.


known limitations
-------------------
- inline emphasis doesn't nest (bold containing italic etc). The
  inline parser is a single flat pass on purpose.
- lists are single-level, no nested indentation.
- tables aren't supported.
- the html mode has no CSS and no box model - it's there to "code
  and see", not to replace a browser.
- inline images/formulas are sized off one hardcoded line-height
  guess rather than shaping properly around ascenders/descenders.

None of these are fundamental; they're just where "still minimal"
won out over "handles everything". Patches welcome.
